Here's a code example of a fictional `Order` microservice in Java, used with Spring Boot:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Value("${microservice.order-base-url}")
    private String orderBaseResource

    public void placeOrder(Order orderDetails) {
        // Create a new RestTemplate instance
        RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

        // Construct the order confirmation URL
        String orderConfirmationUrl = StringUtils.validateURL(orderBaseResource + "/order");

        // Check if the order details contain the URN
        if (StringUtils.isRequired(orderDetails.getUrn())) {
            // Create a new RestProxyJsonRequest instance
            RestProxyJsonRequest restProxyJsonRequest = new RestProxyJsonRequest();

            // Set the URI and JSON content for the request
            restProxyJsonRequest.setUri(orderConfirmationUrl);
            restProxyJsonRequest.setJsonContent(orderDetails);

            // Post the request to the order confirmation URL
            restTemplate.postForObject(orderConfirmationUrl, restProxyJsonRequest);
        } else {
            // Create a new RestProxyRestResponse instance
            RestProxyRestResponse response = new RestProxyRestResponse();

            // Set the request context
            response.setRequestContext(requestContext);

            // Post the orderDetails to the order confirmation URL
            Order placedOrder = restTemplate.postForObject(orderConfirmationUrl, orderDetails, placedOrder); 
        }
    }
}
```

This code demonstrates how to create a new `OrderService` instance with a `placeOrder` method that takes an `Order` object as an argument. The method uses a `RestTemplate` instance to post the order details to the order confirmation URL. If the order details contain the URN, it will use the `RestProxyJsonRequest` instance to set the URI and JSON content for the request.

